DEFINITIONS = [
    ("At-Will Employment", "An employment relationship in which either the employer or the employee may terminate the relationship at any time, for any reason or no reason, with or without notice, except as otherwise limited by applicable law or a written agreement signed by the Chief Executive Officer."),
    ("Exempt Employee", "An employee who meets the salary level, salary basis, and duties tests established under the Fair Labor Standards Act for exemption from minimum wage and overtime pay requirements, typically classified under the executive, administrative, professional, computer, or outside sales exemptions."),
    ("Non-Exempt Employee", "An employee who does not satisfy the FLSA criteria for exemption from overtime and is therefore entitled to receive overtime pay at not less than one and one-half times the regular rate of pay for all hours worked beyond 40 in a single workweek."),
    ("Full-Time Employee", "An employee regularly scheduled to work a minimum of 40 hours per workweek, or such other threshold as may be established by the Company's applicable benefit plan documents for purposes of benefits eligibility."),
    ("Part-Time Employee", "An employee regularly scheduled to work fewer than 40 hours per workweek. Eligibility for specific Company benefits may differ for part-time employees as specified in applicable plan documents and benefit summaries."),
    ("Temporary Employee", "An employee hired for a defined period or specific project whose employment is expected to terminate upon expiration of that period or completion of the project. Temporary employees are generally ineligible for benefits unless expressly stated otherwise."),
    ("Independent Contractor", "An individual engaged by the Company pursuant to a written services agreement who is not classified as an employee, retains control over the manner and means of performing services, and is responsible for their own tax obligations."),
    ("Qualifying Event", "An event defined under applicable federal or state law — including marriage, divorce, birth or adoption of a child, or involuntary loss of other group health coverage — that permits mid-year changes to benefit plan elections outside the annual open enrollment window."),
    ("Serious Health Condition", "As defined under the Family and Medical Leave Act (29 C.F.R. § 825.113), an illness, injury, impairment, or physical or mental condition that involves inpatient care or continuing treatment by a licensed health care provider, including conditions that prevent an employee from performing the essential functions of their job."),
    ("Intermittent Leave", "FMLA or other approved leave taken in separate, non-continuous blocks of time, or through a reduction in the employee's normal weekly or daily work schedule, to address a single qualifying medical or family reason."),
    ("Essential Functions", "The fundamental, core job duties of a position as distinguished from marginal or peripheral functions. Under the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Company is required to provide reasonable accommodation to enable a qualified individual with a disability to perform essential functions unless doing so causes undue hardship."),
    ("Reasonable Accommodation", "Any modification or adjustment to a job, work environment, application process, or standard Company practice that enables a qualified individual with a disability to enjoy equal employment opportunity, provided the accommodation does not impose an undue hardship on Company operations."),
    ("Undue Hardship", "Under the ADA and applicable regulations, an action that requires significant difficulty or expense when considered in light of factors including the Company's financial resources, the nature of operations, the size of the workforce, and the impact on other employees and the overall organization."),
    ("Interactive Process", "A required good-faith dialogue between the Company and an employee or applicant with a known disability for the purpose of identifying effective and feasible reasonable accommodations, required under the ADA and corresponding state law."),
    ("Protected Class", "A group of individuals sharing a characteristic that is protected from employment discrimination under applicable federal, state, or local law, including but not limited to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age (40 and over), disability, genetic information, and veteran status."),
    ("Hostile Work Environment", "A form of unlawful harassment in which conduct based on a protected characteristic is sufficiently severe or pervasive to alter the conditions of employment and create an intimidating, hostile, or offensive work environment, as evaluated from the perspective of a reasonable person in the employee's position."),
    ("Quid Pro Quo Harassment", "A form of unlawful sexual harassment in which submission to or rejection of unwelcome sexual conduct is used, explicitly or implicitly, as the basis for an employment decision, including hiring, promotion, assignment, or termination."),
    ("Retaliation", "Any adverse employment action taken against an employee because the employee engaged in a legally protected activity, including filing a discrimination complaint, participating in an internal or external investigation, or exercising rights under the FMLA, ADA, NLRA, or other applicable statutes."),
    ("Protected Activity", "Conduct expressly protected by law from employer retaliation, including reporting suspected violations of law or Company policy, filing or supporting a charge with the EEOC or a state agency, exercising FMLA rights, or engaging in protected concerted activity under the NLRA."),
    ("Concerted Activity", "Action taken by two or more employees, or by one employee on behalf of others, for their mutual aid or protection regarding the terms and conditions of employment, protected from employer interference or retaliation under Section 7 of the National Labor Relations Act."),
    ("FMLA", "The Family and Medical Leave Act of 1993 (29 U.S.C. §§ 2601–2654), a federal statute requiring covered employers with 50 or more employees to provide eligible employees up to 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave per year for qualifying family and medical reasons."),
    ("FLSA", "The Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 (29 U.S.C. §§ 201–219), the federal statute establishing minimum wage, overtime pay, recordkeeping, and child labor standards applicable to covered employers and employees in the private sector and in federal, state, and local governments."),
    ("ADA", "The Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990 (42 U.S.C. §§ 12101–12213), as amended by the ADA Amendments Act of 2008, prohibiting discrimination against qualified individuals with disabilities and requiring covered employers to engage in the interactive process and provide reasonable accommodations."),
    ("COBRA", "The Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1985, requiring covered employers with 20 or more employees to offer qualified beneficiaries the opportunity to continue group health plan coverage for up to 18, 29, or 36 months following a qualifying event that would otherwise result in loss of coverage."),
    ("ERISA", "The Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 (29 U.S.C. §§ 1001–1461), establishing minimum standards for most voluntarily established employee retirement and health benefit plans in the private sector and providing rules for federal income tax treatment of transactions associated with employee benefit plans."),
    ("HIPAA", "The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996, including the Privacy Rule (45 C.F.R. Part 164) and Security Rule, which establish standards for protecting individuals' medical records and health information maintained by covered entities and their business associates."),
    ("OSHA", "The Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970 (29 U.S.C. § 651 et seq.), establishing the Occupational Safety and Health Administration and imposing on employers a general duty to provide a workplace free from recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm."),
    ("Base Salary", "The fixed annual or hourly compensation paid to an employee in exchange for the performance of job duties, exclusive of bonuses, commissions, overtime premiums, benefits contributions, equity awards, and other variable or supplemental pay components."),
    ("Total Compensation", "The aggregate value of all remuneration provided to an employee, encompassing base salary, variable pay (including bonuses and commissions), employer-paid benefits, retirement plan contributions, equity awards, perquisites, and any other forms of direct or indirect compensation."),
    ("Merit Increase", "A salary adjustment awarded to an employee on the basis of individual performance evaluations and contributions, as distinguished from cost-of-living adjustments, market-rate corrections, or increases tied to promotion or reclassification."),
    ("Vesting", "The process by which an employee earns a nonforfeitable right to employer contributions made to a retirement plan or to equity awards granted by the Company, typically subject to a time-based schedule requiring continued employment through specified vesting dates."),
    ("Cliff Vesting", "A vesting schedule under which an employee becomes fully vested in employer contributions or equity awards on a single date after a specified period of continuous employment, with no vesting occurring before that date."),
    ("Graded Vesting", "A vesting schedule under which an employee's nonforfeitable rights to employer contributions or equity awards accrue incrementally over a defined period, with a specified percentage becoming vested each year until full vesting is achieved."),
    ("HSA", "A Health Savings Account — a tax-advantaged personal savings account available exclusively to individuals enrolled in a qualifying high-deductible health plan (HDHP), used to pay for qualified medical expenses, with contributions rolling over year to year and the account owned by the employee."),
    ("FSA", "A Flexible Spending Account — an employer-sponsored benefit plan allowing employees to elect to set aside pre-tax payroll deductions for eligible healthcare or dependent care expenses, subject to IRS annual contribution limits and, for healthcare FSAs, the use-or-lose rule."),
    ("Open Enrollment", "The annual period designated by the Company during which employees may elect, modify, or waive benefit plan coverage for the upcoming plan year. Outside of open enrollment, changes are generally permitted only upon a qualifying life event."),
    ("Severance", "Compensation and benefits provided by the Company to eligible employees upon involuntary separation, calculated pursuant to the severance schedule in effect at the time of separation and contingent upon execution of a general release of claims in a form acceptable to the Company."),
    ("PTO", "Paid Time Off — the Company's unified paid leave program providing eligible employees with a bank of days that may be used for vacation, personal time, or illness, subject to accrual, carryover, and usage restrictions described in the Leave policy."),
    ("WARN Act", "The Worker Adjustment and Retraining Notification Act of 1988 (29 U.S.C. §§ 2101–2109), requiring employers with 100 or more full-time employees to provide at least 60 calendar days' advance written notice before a covered plant closing or mass layoff affecting the required number of workers."),
    ("Background Check", "A review of an individual's criminal history, employment history, education credentials, professional licenses, and/or credit history conducted through a consumer reporting agency pursuant to the requirements of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, used as part of the employment eligibility process."),
    ("Performance Improvement Plan", "A formal, documented process used to communicate specific performance deficiencies to an employee, establish measurable improvement expectations and timelines, and provide structured support intended to help the employee meet performance standards before further disciplinary action is taken."),
    ("Corrective Action", "Any formal measure taken by the Company in response to an employee's performance deficiency or policy violation, including but not limited to verbal counseling, written warnings, final written warnings, suspension, demotion, and termination of employment."),
    ("Progressive Discipline", "A disciplinary framework that generally escalates through a defined sequence — typically verbal warning, written warning, final written warning, and termination — though the Company reserves the right to skip steps or proceed directly to termination based on the severity or nature of the conduct."),
    ("Grievance", "A formal written complaint submitted by an employee alleging a violation of Company policy, an applicable law, or the terms of an employment agreement, subject to investigation and resolution through the procedures set forth in the Grievance Policy."),
    ("Arbitration", "A form of alternative dispute resolution in which a neutral, third-party arbitrator hears and decides employment-related disputes pursuant to the terms of a written arbitration agreement, providing a final and binding determination outside of the court system."),
    ("Non-Disclosure Agreement", "A legally binding agreement — also referred to as a confidentiality agreement — in which a signatory agrees to maintain in confidence specified categories of information and to refrain from disclosing such information to unauthorized parties."),
    ("Non-Compete Agreement", "A contractual restriction, enforceable under applicable state law, in which an employee agrees not to engage in competitive employment or business activities within a defined geographic area and for a specified period following termination of employment."),
    ("Conflict of Interest", "A situation in which an employee's personal, financial, professional, or familial interests could improperly influence, or reasonably appear to influence, the employee's business judgment or their ability to act in the best interests of the Company."),
    ("Whistleblower", "An employee who, in good faith, reports a suspected violation of applicable law, regulatory requirement, or Company policy to an appropriate internal or external authority, and who is protected from retaliation for making such a report under applicable statutes and Company policy."),
    ("Work Product", "All tangible and intangible outputs — including documents, code, software, designs, inventions, formulas, research, and other creative or technical works — created by an employee within the scope of their employment or using Company resources, and therefore owned by the Company under applicable intellectual property law."),
    ("Confidential Information", "Non-public business, financial, technical, operational, or strategic information belonging to the Company or its clients, the unauthorized disclosure of which could result in competitive disadvantage, financial harm, legal liability, or reputational damage to the Company."),
    ("I-9 Verification", "The process by which an employer verifies the identity and employment authorization of each newly hired employee pursuant to the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986 (8 U.S.C. § 1324a), using Form I-9 and specified acceptable identity and work authorization documents."),
    ("Material Non-Public Information", "Information about a publicly traded company that has not been released to the general investing public and that a reasonable investor would likely consider important in making a decision to buy, sell, or hold that company's securities, the misuse of which constitutes insider trading."),
    ("Constructive Discharge", "A legal doctrine under which an employee's voluntary resignation is treated as an involuntary termination because the employer deliberately made working conditions so intolerable that a reasonable employee in the same situation would have felt compelled to resign."),
    ("Disparate Treatment", "A form of intentional employment discrimination in which an employer treats an individual less favorably than similarly situated employees because of a protected characteristic such as race, sex, religion, or national origin."),
    ("Disparate Impact", "A form of employment discrimination in which a facially neutral employment policy or practice has a statistically significant disproportionate adverse effect on members of a protected class and cannot be justified by business necessity."),
    ("Job Classification", "The systematic process of evaluating and grouping positions with substantially similar duties, responsibilities, required qualifications, and levels of accountability into defined job grades or families for purposes of compensation, benefits administration, and legal compliance."),
    ("Bereavement Leave", "Paid leave provided to eligible employees following the death of an immediate family member or other qualifying relative, as defined in the Company's Leave Policy, to attend memorial services, handle estate matters, and attend to related personal obligations."),
    ("Leave of Absence", "An authorized, temporary period away from active work status, either paid or unpaid, that does not sever the employment relationship, granted pursuant to applicable federal or state law or Company policy for qualifying personal, medical, or family reasons."),
    ("Salary Band", "A defined range of compensation — bounded by a minimum, midpoint, and maximum — established for a particular job level or classification based on internal equity analysis and external market benchmarking data, used to guide hiring and compensation decisions."),
    ("Job Leveling", "The formal process of evaluating positions using defined criteria such as scope of responsibility, required expertise, decision-making authority, and impact on the organization in order to assign each position to a standardized level within the Company's career architecture."),
    ("Successor Liability", "The legal principle under which an employer that acquires or succeeds to the business of another employer may inherit the prior employer's obligations under applicable employment statutes, collective bargaining agreements, and other contracts."),
    ("Pay Equity", "The principle and legal requirement that employees performing substantially equal or comparable work receive equal or comparable pay, regardless of protected characteristics such as sex, race, or national origin, as required by the Equal Pay Act and applicable state laws."),
    ("Data Subject", "Under applicable privacy law, including the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), any identified or identifiable natural person to whom personal data relates and who may exercise defined rights regarding the collection, processing, and use of their personal information."),
    ("Material Adverse Change", "A significant negative development in the business, financial condition, operations, or prospects of the Company that may trigger specific legal obligations, contract provisions, or benefit plan adjustments as specified in applicable agreements or plan documents."),
    ("EEOC Charge", "A formal administrative complaint filed with the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission by an individual alleging a violation of a federal anti-discrimination statute; filing a charge is generally a prerequisite to bringing a civil lawsuit under Title VII, the ADA, the ADEA, and related statutes."),
    ("Exempt Functions Test", "The analysis applied under the FLSA regulations to determine whether an employee's primary duty satisfies the requirements for classification as exempt under the executive, administrative, professional, or other recognized FLSA exemptions."),
    ("Wage Garnishment", "A legal process by which the Company, as employer, is required by court order or administrative directive to withhold a specified portion of an employee's wages and remit those funds to a creditor, governmental agency, or other authorized recipient."),
    # Additional definitions
    ("Accrual Rate", "The rate at which an employee earns paid time off, sick leave, or other leave benefits over time, typically expressed as a number of hours or days earned per pay period, per month, or per year of service."),
    ("Active Employment", "A status in which an employee is performing regularly scheduled work and is not on an approved leave of absence, layoff, or suspension, used as a threshold condition for eligibility under certain benefit plans and policies."),
    ("Adverse Action", "Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, any denial of employment or other employment action taken based in whole or in part on information contained in a consumer report, which triggers specific pre- and post-action notice obligations to the applicant or employee."),
    ("Agency Fee", "In a union-represented workplace, an amount that may be required of non-union employees as a condition of employment to cover the costs of collective bargaining and contract administration, subject to applicable constitutional and statutory limitations."),
    ("Alternative Dispute Resolution", "A range of processes — including mediation, arbitration, and neutral fact-finding — used to resolve employment disputes outside of court litigation, typically faster and at lower cost, and often governed by a written agreement between the parties."),
    ("Annual Performance Review", "The formal, year-end evaluation of an employee's performance against established goals, competencies, and behavioral expectations, used as the primary basis for merit increase decisions, promotion recommendations, and development planning."),
    ("Attestation", "A formal declaration by an employee or authorized representative — typically made in writing and under penalty of perjury or Company policy — confirming the accuracy of information provided, such as timekeeping records or benefit enrollment elections."),
    ("Authorized Representative", "An individual designated in writing by an employee or applicant to act on their behalf in connection with specified Company processes, such as benefit enrollment, leave requests, or participation in an administrative hearing."),
    ("Bargaining Unit", "A group of employees designated by the National Labor Relations Board or by mutual agreement between an employer and a union as sharing a sufficient community of interest to be represented collectively by a single union in collective bargaining."),
    ("Base Pay Rate", "The hourly rate or annual salary assigned to an employee's position exclusive of shift differentials, overtime, bonuses, or other supplemental payments; the primary input into overtime calculations and salary increase computations."),
    ("Benchmarking", "The process of comparing the Company's compensation, benefits, policies, or practices against comparable organizations using survey data from established compensation and HR research providers, used to assess market competitiveness."),
    ("Biometric Identifier", "Physiological or behavioral characteristics — including fingerprints, retinal scans, facial geometry, voiceprints, or gait analysis — used as unique identifying data, subject to state biometric privacy laws including the Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act."),
    ("Blackout Period", "A period, typically in connection with a retirement plan or securities trading, during which certain transactions are temporarily restricted. Under Sarbanes-Oxley, directors and executive officers are prohibited from trading Company securities during defined plan blackout periods."),
    ("Bona Fide Occupational Qualification", "Under Title VII, the ADEA, and applicable state law, a characteristic that is a genuine and necessary qualification for performing a job, constituting a narrow legal defense to an otherwise prohibited employment practice based on sex, religion, national origin, or age."),
    ("Bonus Target", "The amount of variable incentive compensation an employee would receive if all applicable performance metrics are achieved at exactly the target level, expressed as a percentage of base salary or as a fixed dollar amount."),
    ("Burden of Proof", "The obligation of a party in a legal or administrative proceeding to produce sufficient evidence to establish the truth of a claim to the applicable standard of proof; in employment discrimination cases, the burden of proof frameworks are governed by McDonnell Douglas Corp. v. Green."),
    ("Business Necessity", "A legal defense available to an employer in a disparate impact discrimination case, requiring the employer to demonstrate that a challenged employment practice is related to job performance and consistent with business necessity, as defined under Griggs v. Duke Power Co. and 42 U.S.C. § 2000e-2(k)."),
    ("Career Ladder", "A formalized progression of job levels within a job family or functional area, defining the competencies, responsibilities, and performance expectations associated with each successive level, used to support employee development and guide promotion decisions."),
    ("Carryover", "The transfer of unused paid time off or other leave balances from one accrual or benefit year to the next, subject to maximum carryover limits established by Company policy or applicable law."),
    ("Cause for Termination", "Conduct, performance deficiency, or legal violation sufficiently serious to justify immediate termination of employment without prior progressive discipline, including but not limited to willful misconduct, gross insubordination, workplace violence, or criminal activity affecting the employment relationship."),
    ("Change in Control", "An event — such as a merger, acquisition, or sale of substantially all Company assets — that may trigger specific employee rights or obligations under employment agreements, equity plans, or benefit arrangements as defined in the applicable agreement or plan document."),
    ("Charge of Discrimination", "A formal written complaint filed with the EEOC or an applicable state civil rights agency alleging a violation of a federal or state anti-discrimination statute, which initiates the administrative process and is generally a prerequisite to civil litigation."),
    ("Classification Audit", "A systematic review of employee and contractor classifications to ensure that workers are appropriately categorized as employees or independent contractors, and as exempt or non-exempt, for compliance with the FLSA, applicable state law, and tax requirements."),
    ("Clawback", "A contractual provision — required by Dodd-Frank and certain equity plans — under which the Company may recover previously paid incentive compensation from current or former executives if performance results are later found to have been misstated or if other specified triggering events occur."),
    ("Collective Bargaining Agreement", "A written contract negotiated between the Company and a certified labor union representing a defined bargaining unit, governing wages, hours, terms and conditions of employment, and the processes for resolving workplace disputes for the covered employees."),
    ("Compensatory Time", "In lieu of overtime pay, time off at a rate of not less than one and one-half hours for each overtime hour worked, permitted for state and local government employers under the FLSA but generally prohibited for private-sector employers."),
    ("Competency", "A defined cluster of knowledge, skills, abilities, and behaviors that contribute to successful job performance at a specified level, used in performance evaluations, hiring, learning and development programs, and promotion decisions."),
    ("Compliance Audit", "A systematic, documented review of the Company's practices, records, and policies against applicable legal requirements, regulatory standards, and internal policies, used to identify gaps, assess risk, and support corrective action planning."),
    ("Conditional Offer of Employment", "A job offer that is contingent upon the successful completion of specified pre-employment screening steps — such as background checks, drug testing, or reference verification — that must be cleared before the offer becomes final."),
    ("Confidentiality Agreement", "A legally binding contract in which a party agrees to maintain in confidence specified information and to refrain from disclosing it to unauthorized parties; also referred to as a non-disclosure agreement (NDA)."),
    ("Consumer Report", "Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, any written, oral, or other communication by a consumer reporting agency bearing on an individual's creditworthiness, credit standing, credit capacity, character, general reputation, personal characteristics, or mode of living, used for employment purposes."),
    ("Contingent Worker", "A broad category encompassing temporary employees, contractors, freelancers, statement-of-work workers, and others who perform work for the Company without being classified as direct employees, subject to specific management protocols to protect against co-employment liability."),
    ("Cost-of-Living Adjustment", "A compensation increase tied to inflation indices — typically the Consumer Price Index — rather than individual performance, intended to maintain an employee's purchasing power as the cost of living rises; distinguished from merit increases based on performance."),
    ("Covered Entity", "Under HIPAA, a health plan, health care clearinghouse, or health care provider that transmits health information in electronic form in connection with a covered transaction, subject to HIPAA Privacy and Security Rule obligations."),
    ("Defined Benefit Plan", "A retirement plan in which the Company commits to providing a specified monthly benefit at retirement, calculated according to a formula based on factors such as salary history and years of service, with investment risk borne by the plan sponsor."),
    ("Defined Contribution Plan", "A retirement savings plan in which the Company and/or the employee make specified contributions to individual employee accounts, with retirement benefits dependent on account balances at retirement and investment risk borne by the participant."),
    ("Demotion", "A personnel action in which an employee is moved to a position at a lower job level, grade, or compensation band, either as a corrective action in response to performance or conduct deficiencies or as a voluntary arrangement at the employee's request."),
    ("Dependent", "An individual — typically a spouse, domestic partner, or child meeting defined age and other eligibility criteria — who may be enrolled in the employee's employer-sponsored benefit plans as defined in the applicable plan documents."),
    ("Disciplinary Suspension", "A period of unpaid (or, for exempt employees, paid in limited circumstances) absence from work imposed as a formal corrective action in response to a serious or repeated policy violation, generally documented in the employee's personnel file."),
    ("Disclosure", "The release, transfer, provision of access to, or divulgation of information about an employee, applicant, or other individual to any person or entity outside the Company, subject to applicable privacy laws and Company policy restrictions."),
    ("Domestic Partner", "An individual who has entered into a registered domestic partnership or civil union recognized by applicable state or local law, or who satisfies the Company's eligibility requirements for domestic partner benefits as set forth in the applicable benefit plan documents."),
    ("Drug-Free Workplace", "A workplace in which the manufacture, distribution, dispensation, possession, or use of a controlled substance by any employee is prohibited, consistent with the requirements of the Drug-Free Workplace Act of 1988 for federal contractors and grantees."),
    ("Dual Employment", "A situation in which an employee holds a second position — either within the Company or with an external employer — that may create scheduling conflicts, conflicts of interest, or other compliance concerns subject to the Company's Outside Employment policy."),
    ("Duty of Care", "The employer's legal and ethical obligation to take reasonable steps to protect employees from foreseeable harm, including physical hazards in the workplace, known threats of violence, and unsafe working conditions."),
    ("E-Verify", "The federal internet-based system operated by USCIS and SSA that allows enrolled employers to electronically verify the employment eligibility of newly hired employees by comparing I-9 information against federal database records."),
    ("Earned Wage Access", "A benefit program that allows employees to access a portion of wages they have already earned before their scheduled payday, subject to applicable state laws and the terms of the Company's payroll administration program."),
    ("Effective Date", "The date on which a policy, benefit plan, employment action, or other formal Company decision takes legal effect and becomes operative, as distinct from the date of approval or signature."),
    ("Employee Assistance Program", "An employer-sponsored program providing confidential assessment, short-term counseling, referral, and follow-up services for employees and their household family members who have personal or work-related problems affecting their productivity or well-being."),
    ("Employee Self-Service", "A technology-enabled capability allowing employees to view and update their own HR information — including personal data, benefit elections, direct deposit, and leave balances — through a secure online portal without requiring HR intervention."),
    ("Employment at Will", "See At-Will Employment. The default legal status of private-sector employment in Wisconsin and most U.S. states, under which either party may terminate the employment relationship at any time without cause, subject to contractual and statutory exceptions."),
    ("Employment Verification", "The process of confirming an individual's current or prior employment status, dates of employment, job title, and, in limited cases, salary history or eligibility for rehire, typically conducted by prospective employers or creditors through written request."),
    ("Exit Interview", "A structured conversation or survey conducted with a voluntarily separating employee prior to their final day of employment, designed to gather information about the reasons for departure, workplace culture, and management effectiveness to inform retention strategies."),
    ("Fitness for Duty Examination", "A medical evaluation required by the Company under specific circumstances to determine whether an employee who has been absent from work due to a medical condition is physically and mentally capable of returning to and performing the essential functions of their position."),
    ("Flexible Work Arrangement", "A modification to an employee's standard work schedule, location, or hours — including telecommuting, compressed workweeks, job sharing, or flextime — approved by the employee's manager pursuant to the Company's Flexible Work Policy."),
    ("Floating Holiday", "A paid day off that an employee may use at a time of their choosing, subject to manager approval and scheduling requirements, as distinct from a Company-designated holiday on a fixed calendar date."),
    ("Furlough", "A temporary, mandatory, and unpaid reduction in work hours or a mandatory period of leave without pay, typically implemented as a cost-reduction measure in response to business downturns, as distinguished from a layoff, which is a permanent separation."),
    ("Gainsharing", "A group incentive plan under which employees share in the financial gains resulting from improvements in productivity, quality, or cost reduction achieved by a team or business unit, typically paid quarterly or annually from a calculated bonus pool."),
    ("Good Standing", "An employee status indicating that the individual has no active formal disciplinary actions on record, has met applicable performance standards, and is otherwise in compliance with Company policies, used as an eligibility criterion for certain programs and opportunities."),
    ("Grandfather Provision", "A policy term that exempts existing employees or practices from new requirements that would otherwise apply, typically used to protect employees who were hired under or have relied on prior policy standards from adverse retroactive impact."),
    ("Green Card", "The informal name for a Permanent Resident Card (Form I-551), evidencing that a foreign national has been lawfully admitted to the United States for permanent residence and is authorized to work for any employer without visa restrictions."),
    ("Gross Misconduct", "Willful, serious, or flagrant violation of Company policy or applicable law — including workplace violence, fraud, theft, serious safety violations, and sexual assault — that may result in immediate termination and, depending on circumstances, forfeiture of certain post-employment benefits."),
    ("Group Health Plan", "Under ERISA and the ACA, a plan or program maintained by an employer to provide health benefits to employees and their dependents, subject to COBRA continuation coverage, ACA employer mandate, mental health parity, and other federal statutory requirements."),
    ("Hardship Withdrawal", "A distribution from a 401(k) or other qualified retirement plan taken to address an immediate and heavy financial need — such as medical expenses, prevention of eviction, or funeral expenses — under IRS guidelines; generally subject to income tax and early withdrawal penalties."),
    ("Health Reimbursement Arrangement", "An employer-funded account under which the Company reimburses employees for qualified medical expenses up to a specified annual maximum, with unused balances potentially carrying forward at the employer's discretion; subject to IRS and ACA requirements."),
    ("Highly Compensated Employee", "Under ERISA and the Internal Revenue Code, an employee who owns more than 5% of the Company or earned compensation above the applicable IRS threshold in the prior plan year, subject to annual plan non-discrimination testing requirements."),
    ("Imputed Income", "The fair market value of a non-cash employer-provided benefit that must be included in an employee's gross income for federal income tax purposes, such as group-term life insurance coverage above $50,000 or the value of a Company vehicle used for personal purposes."),
    ("Insubordination", "An employee's direct and willful refusal to follow a reasonable, lawful directive from a supervisor or manager, constituting conduct subject to progressive discipline or, depending on severity, immediate termination."),
    ("Job Abandonment", "A presumptive voluntary resignation arising when an employee fails to report to work for a consecutive number of scheduled days — typically three or more — without notifying their supervisor or Human Resources and without an approved leave."),
    ("Job Description", "A written document describing the essential functions, required qualifications, physical requirements, and reporting relationships of a position, used in hiring, performance management, compensation analysis, and ADA accommodation assessments."),
    ("Joint Employer", "A legal finding that two or more entities exercise sufficient control over the terms and conditions of employment of shared workers to be held jointly liable as co-employers under the FLSA, NLRA, Title VII, or other statutes; a risk that arises particularly in staffing agency and subcontracting relationships."),
    ("Key Employee", "Under the FMLA, a salaried employee who is among the highest-paid 10% of employees within a 75-mile radius of their worksite, whose reinstatement after leave may be denied if it would cause substantial and grievous economic injury to the Company."),
    ("Knowledge Worker", "An employee whose primary job function involves the creation, analysis, application, or distribution of specialized information or expertise, as distinguished from manual or physical labor; typically but not exclusively classified as FLSA-exempt."),
    ("Labor Market", "The geographic and occupational context in which the Company competes for talent, used as the reference point for external pay benchmarking, equity analysis, and determination of competitive compensation ranges."),
    ("Lateral Transfer", "A personnel action in which an employee moves to a different position at the same job level and compensation band, without promotion or demotion, for purposes of career development, operational need, or organizational restructuring."),
    ("Last-Chance Agreement", "A written agreement between the Company and an employee who has committed a serious policy violation, providing the employee with a final opportunity to avoid termination subject to defined conditions of conduct, performance, and compliance over a specified period."),
    ("Layoff", "An involuntary, permanent separation from employment resulting from elimination of a position due to organizational restructuring, business closure, reduction in force, or other non-disciplinary business reasons, as distinguished from termination for cause."),
    ("Leave Abuse", "The use of approved leave for purposes inconsistent with the qualifying reason for which it was granted, or misrepresentation of the need for leave, constituting a policy violation subject to corrective action up to and including termination."),
    ("Liaison", "An employee designated to facilitate communication or coordination between two organizational units, between the Company and an external agency, or between management and employees on a defined subject matter."),
    ("Look-Back Period", "Under the ACA, a defined measurement period used by applicable large employers to determine whether variable-hour and seasonal employees worked an average of 30 or more hours per week and are therefore eligible for employer-sponsored health coverage."),
    ("Mandatory Arbitration", "An employer-employee agreement requiring that specified categories of employment disputes be resolved through binding arbitration rather than through court litigation, subject to applicable enforceability requirements under the Federal Arbitration Act and state law."),
    ("Market Adjustment", "A compensation increase made to align an employee's pay with current market rates for their role, as identified through external benchmarking, rather than reflecting individual performance; distinct from a merit increase."),
    ("Mediation", "A voluntary, non-binding dispute resolution process in which a trained neutral third party facilitates negotiated resolution between parties to a workplace dispute, used as an alternative or precursor to formal investigation or arbitration."),
    ("Medical Certification", "Documentation provided by a licensed health care provider confirming that an employee or family member has a serious health condition qualifying for FMLA or other leave, including the nature of the condition, expected duration, and functional limitations."),
    ("Minimum Wage", "The lowest hourly rate that employers are legally permitted to pay covered employees, established by the federal FLSA and, in Wisconsin, by Wis. Stat. § 104.01 et seq.; where federal and state rates differ, the higher rate applies."),
    ("Moonlighting", "An employee's secondary employment or business activity engaged in outside of their primary work schedule for the Company, subject to the Company's Outside Employment policy and conflict of interest restrictions."),
    ("Net Pay", "The amount of compensation received by an employee after all required and voluntary deductions — including federal and state income tax withholding, FICA taxes, health insurance premiums, retirement plan contributions, and other elected deductions — have been subtracted from gross pay."),
    ("Non-Solicitation Agreement", "A contractual restriction in which an employee agrees not to solicit the Company's employees, clients, or business partners for a competing employer or for the employee's own competing business for a defined period following separation from employment."),
    ("On-Call Time", "Time during which an employee is required to remain available to report to work on short notice, which may constitute compensable work time under the FLSA depending on the degree of restriction placed on the employee's activities and freedom of movement."),
    ("Paid Family Leave", "A benefit program providing wage replacement during leave taken to care for a new child, a seriously ill family member, or address a qualifying military exigency; required by law in certain states and provided voluntarily by some employers beyond legal requirements."),
    ("Payroll Period", "The recurring interval of time — typically weekly, biweekly, semimonthly, or monthly — used to calculate, process, and distribute employee compensation; Wisconsin law requires wages to be paid at least twice per month under Wis. Stat. § 109.03."),
    ("Pension", "A defined benefit retirement plan providing lifetime monthly income to eligible retirees based on a formula incorporating years of service and compensation history; increasingly uncommon in the private sector, having been largely replaced by defined contribution plans."),
    ("Performance Goal", "A specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) objective established for an employee at the beginning of a performance period, against which the employee's contributions will be evaluated during the annual or mid-year performance review."),
    ("Phantom Stock", "A form of deferred compensation that tracks the value of Company stock and pays cash or stock equivalents to employees at a future date, providing economic exposure to Company equity without actual share ownership or dilution."),
    ("Plan Administrator", "The entity or individual responsible for the day-to-day operation and administration of an employee benefit plan, including eligibility determinations, claims processing, participant communications, and regulatory filing requirements under ERISA."),
    ("Plan Document", "The formal written instrument governing the terms of an employee benefit plan, including eligibility, coverage, claims procedures, and termination provisions; the controlling document in the event of a conflict with the Summary Plan Description."),
    ("Plan Year", "The 12-month period used for administering a benefit plan, which may coincide with or differ from the calendar year; benefit elections, contribution limits, and non-discrimination testing are generally tied to the plan year."),
    ("Policy Acknowledgment", "A formal declaration by an employee — typically obtained through HR systems at onboarding and at the time of significant policy updates — confirming that the employee has read, understands, and agrees to comply with a specified Company policy."),
    ("Post-Offer Medical Examination", "A physical or psychological examination required of all individuals in a job classification after a conditional offer of employment has been made, which must be job-related and consistent with business necessity under the ADA."),
    ("Predetermination Hearing", "A meeting offered to an employee before the imposition of a significant disciplinary action — particularly termination — to provide an opportunity to respond to allegations and present relevant information, used to support procedural fairness."),
    ("Premium Pay", "Compensation paid at a rate above the employee's regular rate of pay, including overtime premiums, shift differentials, holiday pay, and on-call premiums, as authorized by Company policy or required by applicable law."),
    ("Probationary Period", "A defined initial period of employment — typically 60 to 90 days — during which a newly hired employee's performance and suitability for continued employment are formally evaluated; Company policy may restrict certain benefits or rights during this period."),
    ("Qualified Plan", "A retirement savings plan that meets the requirements of the Internal Revenue Code for favorable tax treatment, including employer deductibility of contributions, tax-deferred growth of plan assets, and employee deferral of income tax on contributions."),
    ("Rate of Pay", "An employee's compensation expressed as an hourly, daily, weekly, or annual amount, used as the basis for overtime calculations, leave pay, and severance computations."),
    ("Reasonable Cause", "The standard applied by the EEOC in determining whether there is sufficient evidence to support a finding that an employer has violated a federal anti-discrimination statute, required as a prerequisite to EEOC-initiated conciliation or litigation."),
    ("Record Retention", "The obligation to preserve employment-related documents — including personnel files, payroll records, benefit plan documents, I-9 forms, and training records — for minimum periods specified by FLSA, ERISA, ADEA, NLRA, and other applicable statutes."),
    ("Reduction in Force", "A planned reduction in the number of employees through layoffs, voluntary separation programs, or other non-disciplinary means, triggered by business conditions, organizational restructuring, or strategic priorities, and subject to WARN Act obligations above specified thresholds."),
    ("Reference Check", "The process of verifying information provided by a job applicant and gathering additional information about the applicant's qualifications, work history, and professional conduct from former supervisors or colleagues, subject to FCRA requirements when conducted through a consumer reporting agency."),
    ("Regular Rate of Pay", "Under the FLSA, the hourly rate used as the basis for calculating overtime premiums, computed by dividing total compensation in a workweek by total hours worked, and generally excluding certain payments such as gifts, expense reimbursements, and discretionary bonuses."),
    ("Resignation", "The voluntary, employee-initiated termination of the employment relationship, effective on a date communicated by the employee, subject to any contractual notice requirement and the Company's right to accept an immediate departure in lieu of a notice period."),
    ("Retaliation", "Any adverse employment action taken against an employee because the employee engaged in a legally protected activity, as defined above; the most frequently filed category of charge with the EEOC in recent years."),
    ("Right to Work", "A state law provision, not applicable in Wisconsin, prohibiting union security agreements that require employees to join a union or pay union fees as a condition of employment; distinguished from at-will employment, with which it is commonly confused."),
    ("Safe Harbor", "A legal or regulatory provision that protects an employer from liability if specified conditions are met, such as the FLSA 'window of correction' safe harbor for pay basis violations or the sexual harassment affirmative defense established in Faragher and Ellerth."),
    ("Salary History Ban", "A prohibition, enacted in a growing number of states and localities, on employer inquiries into or use of an applicant's prior compensation history in making hiring or compensation decisions; not currently required by Wisconsin state law but adopted by Company policy."),
    ("Section 125 Plan", "See Cafeteria Plan. A benefit plan meeting the requirements of 26 U.S.C. § 125 that allows employees to choose between cash and qualified non-taxable benefits, with employee contributions made on a pre-tax basis through payroll deduction."),
    ("Separation Agreement", "A written agreement between the Company and a separating employee that typically includes a general release of claims, confidentiality obligations, and non-disparagement provisions in exchange for severance pay or other consideration beyond what is legally required."),
    ("Skills Assessment", "A structured evaluation of an employee's current technical, functional, or behavioral capabilities against the requirements of their current or target role, used for development planning, reskilling program placement, and succession management."),
    ("Standard of Conduct", "A defined expectation for employee behavior — derived from applicable law, Company values, and workplace norms — the violation of which may result in corrective action under the Company's disciplinary policy."),
    ("Statute of Limitations", "The legally prescribed period within which a claim must be filed or an action commenced; under Title VII, ADEA, and ADA, employees must file EEOC charges within 180 or 300 days of the alleged discriminatory act (depending on state agency availability)."),
    ("Subsidized Benefit", "A benefit for which the Company pays all or a portion of the cost, reducing the employee's out-of-pocket premium or contribution; the degree of employer subsidy is a significant factor in the total compensation value proposition."),
    ("Succession Planning", "The systematic process of identifying and developing internal candidates to fill critical organizational roles in the event of planned or unplanned leadership vacancies, including through structured development assignments, rotational programs, and mentoring."),
    ("Summary Annual Report", "An annual disclosure required by ERISA that provides benefit plan participants with a simplified summary of the plan's annual financial report (Form 5500), including the value of plan assets and changes thereto."),
    ("Summary of Benefits and Coverage", "A standardized, plain-language document required under the ACA that describes the key features of a group health plan, including covered benefits, cost-sharing, and coverage limitations, in a uniform format to facilitate comparison across plans."),
    ("Summary Plan Description", "The primary disclosure document required by ERISA that explains benefit plan terms to participants in understandable language, including eligibility, benefits, claims procedures, ERISA rights, and contact information for the plan administrator."),
    ("Supervisory Employee", "An employee with authority to hire, fire, discipline, evaluate, assign, or effectively recommend such actions with respect to other employees, or whose recommendations on such matters are given particular weight; distinguished from non-supervisory employees for NLRA and other legal purposes."),
    ("Termination for Cause", "An involuntary separation initiated by the Company in response to documented misconduct, performance failure, or policy violation, as distinguished from a reduction in force or other non-disciplinary separation."),
    ("Time and Attendance System", "The technology platform used to record and manage employee work hours, leave usage, and scheduling, and which generates payroll input data; records maintained by this system constitute legally required payroll records under the FLSA."),
    ("Total Rewards", "The complete package of compensation, benefits, learning and development opportunities, well-being programs, and work-life flexibility provided to employees, used as a framework for designing, communicating, and benchmarking the full value of employment."),
    ("Trade Secret", "Information — including formulas, patterns, compilations, programs, devices, methods, techniques, or processes — that derives independent economic value from being secret and that the Company takes reasonable measures to protect from disclosure; protected under the Defend Trade Secrets Act and the Wisconsin Uniform Trade Secrets Act."),
    ("Tuition Reimbursement", "A benefit under which the Company reimburses eligible employees for qualified education expenses — including tuition, fees, and required textbooks — for courses taken at an accredited institution, subject to the annual limits and conditions described in the Education Assistance Policy."),
    ("Unemployment Compensation", "State-administered wage replacement benefits available to eligible workers who lose employment through no fault of their own, funded by employer payroll taxes; in Wisconsin, administered by the Department of Workforce Development under Wis. Stat. § 108.01 et seq."),
    ("Unfair Labor Practice", "A specific employer or union action prohibited by the National Labor Relations Act, including employer interference with protected concerted activity, discrimination against union supporters, and refusal to bargain in good faith with a certified union representative."),
    ("Vested Benefit", "A benefit to which an employee has earned an unconditional legal right, typically through completion of a required service period, and which cannot be forfeited upon termination of employment, including vested retirement plan balances and earned but unused PTO in states that require accrued vacation payout."),
    ("Waiver", "A voluntary, informed relinquishment of a known legal right or remedy, typically required to be in writing; in the employment context, most commonly arising in severance and separation agreements subject to OWBPA requirements for employees age 40 and over."),
    ("Whistleblower Protection Program", "The formal framework — including confidential reporting channels, anti-retaliation policies, and investigation procedures — established to encourage and protect the reporting of suspected violations of law or Company policy, consistent with the Dodd-Frank Act, SOX, and other applicable statutes."),
    ("Work Authorization", "Legal permission to work in the United States as evidenced by an I-9 acceptable document or as confirmed through E-Verify, required for all employees hired after November 6, 1986 regardless of citizenship, national origin, or immigration status."),
    ("Worker Classification", "The determination of whether a worker is an employee or an independent contractor for tax, benefits, and legal purposes, governed by multifactor tests applied by the IRS, DOL, NLRB, and state agencies, with significant liability consequences for misclassification."),
    ("Workers' Compensation", "A state-mandated insurance program providing no-fault medical benefits and wage replacement to employees who suffer work-related injuries or illnesses, in exchange for employees' general agreement not to sue the employer for negligence; administered in Wisconsin under Wis. Stat. § 102."),
    ("Workweek", "Under the FLSA, a fixed, regularly recurring period of 168 hours — seven consecutive 24-hour periods — established by the employer and used as the basis for overtime calculations; the Company's workweek runs from Sunday at 12:00 a.m. through Saturday at 11:59 p.m."),
    ("Zero Tolerance", "A policy approach under which certain categories of misconduct — typically involving workplace violence, harassment, discrimination, or fraud — result in immediate termination of employment upon a finding of violation, without application of the standard progressive discipline sequence."),
]

POLICY_NOTES = [
    ("Important", "Employees who have questions about whether a specific situation is covered by this policy should contact Human Resources before taking action. Acting without guidance when the policy's application is unclear may result in an unintended and correctable violation."),
    ("Note", "This policy is reviewed annually. The version published on the HR intranet is always the most current. Printed copies may become outdated; employees should confirm currency before relying on a printed version for compliance decisions."),
    ("Exception", "The Chief Human Resources Officer may grant written exceptions to specific provisions of this policy where operational necessity or legal requirements make strict compliance impracticable. All exceptions must be documented in writing and retained in the HR policy exception log."),
    ("Important", "Violations of this policy discovered during an HR compliance audit may be addressed retroactively. The fact that a violation went undetected does not diminish the Company's right to take corrective action upon discovery."),
    ("Note", "Where applicable law provides greater protections than those described in this policy, the legal requirement governs. Employees should not assume that this policy represents the ceiling of their rights or the floor of the Company's obligations."),
    ("Reminder", "Managers are reminded that their personal conduct in administering this policy is subject to review. Inconsistent or discriminatory application of policy provisions constitutes an independent violation subject to corrective action."),
    ("Exception", "Employees covered by a written employment agreement signed by the Chief Executive Officer may have rights or obligations that differ from those described in this policy. Such employees should consult their employment agreement and contact HR with questions."),
    ("Important", "Good-faith reporting of suspected policy violations is always protected. Employees who raise concerns in good faith will not be subjected to adverse action for making the report, even if the resulting investigation does not substantiate the underlying concern."),
    ("Note", "The Company's obligation to provide reasonable accommodations under the ADA and applicable state law extends to this policy. Employees who require an accommodation in connection with any provision of this policy should contact Human Resources to initiate the interactive process."),
    ("Reminder", "Documentation is critical. Employees and managers are encouraged to maintain contemporaneous written records of any incident, conversation, or decision that may be relevant to future policy enforcement, investigation, or litigation."),
    ("Important", "This policy applies to all Company-sponsored events and activities, including those held outside of normal business hours or off Company premises. Employees are expected to comply with this policy whenever acting in any capacity that could be perceived as representing the Company."),
    ("Note", "Electronic acknowledgments obtained through the Company's HR system are legally equivalent to handwritten signatures for purposes of policy acknowledgment. Employees who object to electronic signature may submit a paper acknowledgment to Human Resources."),
    ("Exception", "Certain provisions of this policy may be modified for employees working in states with laws more protective than the federal standards described herein. Employees in those states will receive state-specific supplemental guidance from the Human Resources Department."),
    ("Reminder", "This policy is not a substitute for professional legal, financial, or medical advice. Employees with specific legal or financial questions arising from their employment situation should consult with independently retained qualified professionals."),
    ("Important", "Retaliation against any employee for exercising a right protected under this policy or applicable law is itself a serious policy violation subject to corrective action, up to and including termination of the retaliating party's employment."),
    ("Note", "The Company conducts regular audits of policy compliance across all business units. Units with elevated rates of policy violations or complaints may be subject to enhanced monitoring, mandatory training, or leadership review."),
    ("Reminder", "All employees, including senior leaders and executives, are subject to the provisions of this policy without exception. The Company does not recognize informal exemptions based on seniority, title, tenure, or personal relationships with management."),
    ("Exception", "This policy may be updated at any time to reflect changes in applicable law or Company operations. Updates take effect on the date of issuance and are binding regardless of whether an individual employee has completed a new written acknowledgment."),
    ("Important", "Employees on approved leave status remain subject to the obligations imposed by this policy to the extent consistent with the nature and terms of their leave. An absence from active work does not suspend an employee's policy obligations."),
    ("Note", "The Human Resources Department maintains a log of all formal policy exceptions granted under this policy. This log is subject to review by the Board of Directors on request and is reviewed annually by the Chief Human Resources Officer."),
    ("Reminder", "Employees who are unsure whether their intended conduct complies with this policy are strongly encouraged to ask before acting. The cost of a brief consultation with Human Resources is far lower than the cost of a correctable policy violation."),
    ("Important", "This policy creates enforceable obligations for both the Company and its employees. The Company's obligations under this policy may be enforced by employees through the internal complaint process and, where applicable, through external legal and regulatory channels."),
    ("Note", "Where this policy references forms, checklists, or supplemental procedures, those documents are updated independently of the main policy text and may be revised without triggering a formal policy amendment. The most current version of any referenced document controls."),
    ("Exception", "Employees in managerial or supervisory roles may be subject to additional obligations beyond those stated in this policy. Those obligations are described in the Manager Compliance Guide, available from Human Resources upon request."),
    ("Reminder", "The Company's whistleblower protection program applies to any report made in good faith under this policy. Reports submitted through the Company's anonymous ethics hotline receive the same legal and policy protections as reports made through direct HR channels."),
    ("Important", "Employees who believe they have experienced a violation of this policy retain the right to escalate their complaint to the Chief Human Resources Officer or, if necessary, to the Company's Board of Directors if they are dissatisfied with the outcome of the initial investigation."),
    ("Note", "This policy has been reviewed by employment counsel licensed to practice in Wisconsin and reflects applicable state and federal law as of the effective date. The Company will update this policy when material changes to applicable law occur."),
    ("Reminder", "Policy compliance is a shared responsibility. Human Resources sets the framework, managers apply it consistently and fairly, and employees are accountable for their own conduct. All three elements are necessary for a functional and lawful workplace."),
    ("Exception", "Temporary workers placed through staffing agencies are subject to the terms of their agency agreement and to this policy to the extent specified in the applicable vendor agreement. Questions about applicable standards should be directed to both the staffing agency and the Company's HR Department."),
    ("Important", "Nothing in this policy limits the Company's rights under applicable law, including its right to monitor workplace activity on Company systems, investigate suspected violations, and take appropriate corrective action."),
    ("Note", "The Company's Employee Assistance Program provides free, confidential counseling services that may be relevant to situations arising under this policy. Employees are encouraged to contact the EAP before situations escalate to the formal complaint stage."),
    ("Reminder", "Managers who receive a report of a suspected policy violation have an independent obligation to escalate it to Human Resources within two (2) business days, regardless of whether the manager personally believes the reported conduct occurred or rises to the level of a violation."),
    ("Exception", "In jurisdictions where specific provisions of this policy conflict with applicable local ordinance, the more protective standard applies. Human Resources maintains a current list of jurisdiction-specific policy modifications available to employees on request."),
    ("Important", "The Company will not use an employee's prior protected complaint history as a negative factor in performance evaluations, promotion decisions, or any other employment action. To do so constitutes retaliation prohibited by this policy and by applicable federal and state law."),
    ("Note", "Employees who separate from the Company retain the right to file charges with the EEOC or applicable state civil rights agency for alleged violations that occurred during their employment, subject to applicable statutes of limitations."),
    # Additional policy notes
    ("Caution", "This policy contains provisions that may be interpreted differently depending on an employee's classification, work location, or applicable collective bargaining agreement. Employees should confirm which provisions apply to their specific situation with Human Resources before taking action."),
    ("Important", "Managers who are uncertain whether a contemplated action complies with this policy must pause and seek guidance from Human Resources or the Office of General Counsel before proceeding. Proceeding in the absence of that guidance does not mitigate the Company's or the manager's liability."),
    ("Note", "This policy applies equally to all forms of work — in-person, remote, hybrid, and field-based. The fact that work is performed outside of a Company facility does not alter the employee's obligations under this or any other Company policy."),
    ("Reminder", "Policy violations discovered through automated monitoring of Company systems are treated with the same seriousness as violations discovered through human observation or reported complaints. Employees should not assume that the absence of a human observer changes the applicable standard."),
    ("Exception", "International employees and employees working on assignment outside of the United States are subject to locally applicable employment law, which may differ materially from the provisions of this policy. Such employees should contact HR Global Mobility for location-specific guidance."),
    ("Caution", "Employees who engage in conduct that would violate this policy on personal time — including on personal social media — may be subject to corrective action if that conduct has a direct, demonstrable adverse impact on the Company's legitimate business interests or on the work environment of affected colleagues."),
    ("Important", "This policy does not create a contract of employment or alter the at-will nature of the employment relationship. Nothing in this document should be construed as a guarantee of continued employment or as limiting the Company's right to terminate employment at any time."),
    ("Note", "The Company's obligation to investigate complaints under this policy is triggered by actual or constructive knowledge of a potential violation. Employees who are aware of a potential violation are encouraged — and in some roles, required — to report it rather than assuming someone else will do so."),
    ("Reminder", "Confidentiality protections in this policy apply to information obtained during investigations, not to the underlying facts known by participants before the investigation began. Employees may not be prohibited from discussing their own experiences."),
    ("Exception", "Employees who are also attorneys licensed to practice law are reminded that this policy does not supersede their professional obligations under applicable Rules of Professional Conduct. To the extent of any conflict, professional responsibility obligations control."),
    ("Caution", "Provisions of this policy involving mandatory reporting timelines are strictly enforced. Late reports may impair the Company's ability to conduct a timely investigation and may result in the loss of evidence, witness recollection, or legal remedies."),
    ("Important", "Access to another employee's personal, medical, or personnel information obtained incidentally in the course of job duties is subject to strict confidentiality obligations. Unauthorized disclosure constitutes a serious policy violation regardless of whether the disclosure was intentional."),
    ("Note", "This policy has been designed to comply with Wisconsin state law as of its effective date. However, municipal ordinances in specific cities where the Company operates may impose additional requirements. Employees in those locations will receive supplemental notice from HR."),
    ("Reminder", "The existence of this policy does not obligate the Company to take any specific action in any specific case; it establishes a framework and minimum standards. The appropriate response to a given situation always requires judgment informed by the specific facts."),
    ("Exception", "Where this policy imposes a notice or timeline requirement on the employee, and the employee is physically or legally incapacitated in a way that prevents timely compliance, the Company will apply a reasonable accommodation to the notice requirement consistent with applicable law."),
    ("Caution", "Third parties — including clients, vendors, contractors, and visitors — who engage in conduct that would violate this policy on Company premises or in connection with Company activities are subject to removal, contract termination, or other appropriate action by the Company."),
    ("Important", "Employees who obtain information suggesting that a supervisor, manager, or Human Resources representative has violated this policy are encouraged to report the concern to a more senior manager, the Chief Human Resources Officer, or through the anonymous ethics hotline."),
    ("Note", "This policy is designed to work in conjunction with, not in lieu of, the Company's disciplinary, investigation, and grievance policies. Employees should be familiar with those related policies, which are available on the Company intranet and from Human Resources."),
    ("Reminder", "The Company's legal obligations under this policy do not depend on whether the affected employee has formally invoked their rights. Managers are responsible for applying applicable legal protections to all employees regardless of whether those employees are aware of or have asserted those protections."),
    ("Exception", "Executive officers and members of the Board of Directors are subject to additional policies governing their conduct that supplement the provisions of this handbook. Those individuals should consult the Company's Code of Ethics for Senior Financial Officers and the Board Governance Policy."),
    ("Caution", "Employees who receive a written communication from an attorney purporting to assert rights on behalf of a current or former employee in connection with matters covered by this policy must immediately forward that communication to the Office of General Counsel without substantive response."),
    ("Important", "Digital communications — including emails, instant messages, and posts to internal collaboration platforms — that relate to matters covered by this policy may be subject to legal hold obligations and are therefore subject to preservation requirements under the Company's Litigation Hold Policy."),
    ("Note", "All Company training programs related to this policy are tracked by the Learning Management System. Completion of required training is a condition of continued employment, and delinquent completion is subject to the same corrective action framework as other policy compliance failures."),
    ("Reminder", "The Company's insurance coverage for employment practices liability is contingent on the maintenance of documented policies, investigation procedures, and training programs consistent with the requirements of this policy. Departures from these requirements may affect coverage for individual claims."),
    ("Exception", "Notwithstanding any other provision of this policy, the Company's obligations to provide a reasonable accommodation under the ADA and applicable state law take precedence over any conflicting standard or timeline. HR will advise on the applicable accommodation framework on a case-by-case basis."),
    ("Caution", "Employees who assist in the investigation of a complaint under this policy — including as witnesses — are protected from retaliation regardless of the outcome of the investigation. Concerns about potential retaliation should be reported immediately to Human Resources or the ethics hotline."),
    ("Important", "Records created in connection with the administration of this policy — including investigation notes, corrective action documentation, and medical certification records — are maintained in accordance with the Company's Record Retention Schedule and are not available to unauthorized personnel."),
    ("Note", "Employees are encouraged to review related policies, including the Code of Conduct, Conflict of Interest, and Disciplinary Policies, to understand the full framework within which this policy operates. Isolated reading of any single policy may produce an incomplete picture of the applicable standards."),
    ("Reminder", "The standards established in this policy reflect the minimum acceptable level of conduct. Employees who consistently operate at the minimum are not thereby insulated from performance management feedback that their conduct, while technically policy-compliant, does not meet the expectations associated with their role."),
    ("Exception", "Where an employee's physician has placed work restrictions that conflict with the duties required under a specific policy provision, the Company will apply the ADA interactive process to determine whether a reasonable accommodation is available that allows compliance with the spirit of the policy."),
    ("Caution", "Employees who are subject to a final written warning or last-chance agreement are on notice that any additional policy violation — including violations of policies unrelated to the one that precipitated the formal action — may result in immediate termination of employment."),
    ("Important", "The provisions of this policy relating to confidential information apply indefinitely following separation from employment. Former employees who have questions about continuing obligations under this policy should consult with their own legal counsel."),
    ("Note", "Compliance with this policy does not guarantee favorable employment decisions. Policy compliance is a necessary but not sufficient condition for retention, promotion, or other positive employment outcomes; performance expectations apply independently."),
    ("Reminder", "Training on this policy is provided at onboarding and refreshed at least annually. Employees who have not completed required training are not thereby exempted from the policy's requirements; the obligation to comply precedes and exists independently of training completion."),
    ("Exception", "Leaves of absence that occur during performance improvement plan periods toll the PIP clock for the duration of the absence. PIP timelines resume upon the employee's return to active work status without reset of prior progress or warnings."),
    ("Caution", "The anonymous ethics hotline is operated by an independent third-party vendor and does not record caller identification information. Reports made through the hotline are logged in a centralized case management system and routed to the appropriate investigation function within two business days."),
    ("Important", "Employees in positions with elevated access to sensitive Company or customer data are subject to enhanced background screening, additional policy acknowledgment requirements, and periodic access reviews as specified in the Information Security Policy."),
    ("Note", "Supervisors who believe that a team member's personal circumstances may be affecting their work are encouraged to reach out with empathy and to direct the employee to available Company resources, including the Employee Assistance Program, before performance documentation is initiated."),
    ("Reminder", "Corrective action records are maintained in the employee's personnel file for the periods specified in the Record Retention Schedule. Active warning letters are generally considered in subsequent disciplinary decisions for a period of 12 to 24 months, as specified in the Disciplinary Policy."),
    ("Exception", "Employees who are actively engaged in an investigation as complainants or subjects may have their scheduled performance reviews temporarily delayed by up to 90 days to prevent any appearance that the review process is being used to retaliate or reward relative to investigation outcomes."),
    ("Caution", "The Company's monitoring of electronic communications conducted on Company systems is permissible without employee consent under applicable federal and Wisconsin law. Employees should have no expectation of privacy when using Company equipment, networks, or platforms for personal communications."),
    ("Important", "Any modification to this policy that is proposed at the departmental level must be submitted to the Chief Human Resources Officer for approval before it is communicated to employees or applied to any employment decision. Unauthorized departmental modifications are void and unenforceable."),
    ("Note", "Employees who believe this policy conflicts with a right granted under their written employment agreement should contact Human Resources in writing within five business days of identifying the conflict. The Company will review and provide written clarification within fifteen business days."),
    ("Reminder", "The Company publishes a policy changelog on the HR intranet summarizing material changes made in each policy revision cycle. Employees are encouraged to subscribe to policy update notifications to ensure they are aware of changes as they occur."),
    ("Exception", "Employees in roles designated as safety-sensitive — including but not limited to positions requiring a commercial driver's license, operation of heavy machinery, or direct patient care — are subject to additional policy requirements and regulatory standards beyond those described in this general policy."),
    ("Caution", "Failure to respond to a reasonable request for information or participation in an investigation under this policy within the timeframe specified by Human Resources may itself constitute an independent policy violation subject to corrective action."),
    ("Important", "This policy does not create third-party beneficiary rights. Clients, contractors, and other non-employees may not invoke the protections of this policy as a basis for claims against the Company; their recourse is through applicable legal channels and, where applicable, contractual terms."),
    ("Note", "The Company's obligation to maintain policy confidentiality during investigations does not extend indefinitely. Confidential information may be disclosed as required by law, in response to valid legal process, or to the extent necessary to administer a final corrective action."),
    ("Reminder", "All employees are encouraged to complete the voluntary annual policy compliance certification, which confirms that employees have reviewed, understood, and complied with the Company's major HR policies during the preceding calendar year. Completion is tracked through HR Direct."),
    ("Exception", "Acquisitions and newly integrated subsidiaries may operate under transition-period policies for up to 18 months following the closing of an acquisition, subject to the approval of the Chief Human Resources Officer. Employees in those units will be notified of their applicable policy framework at the time of integration."),
    ("Caution", "Employees whose off-duty conduct becomes the subject of a criminal investigation or proceeding are encouraged to promptly notify their HR Business Partner, as such matters may independently affect their employment status depending on the nature of the alleged conduct and their role."),
    ("Important", "Managers who fail to escalate policy violations to Human Resources promptly may be held individually accountable for the resulting harm to affected employees or to the organization. Management accountability is an explicit element of the Company's policy enforcement framework."),
    ("Note", "This policy constitutes part of the consideration for employment. By accepting or continuing employment, employees acknowledge their obligation to comply with this policy and with all future revisions made pursuant to the amendment procedures set forth herein."),
    ("Reminder", "Policy provisions that require employee consent — such as biometric data collection, drug testing, or voluntary disclosure of medical information — must be administered through documented consent processes managed by Human Resources. Line managers may not independently solicit such consents."),
    ("Exception", "Former employees who are re-hired within 90 days of separation may, at HR's discretion, be credited with prior service for purposes of benefit plan eligibility and vesting, depending on the terms of their re-hire offer and applicable plan documents."),
    ("Caution", "Employees who discover or suspect a data security incident that may involve personal information should report the incident immediately to the IT Security team and Human Resources, rather than attempting to investigate or remediate the incident independently."),
    ("Important", "The Company reserves the right to conduct unannounced audits of policy compliance, including review of timekeeping records, expense submissions, conflicts of interest disclosures, and compliance training completion, as part of its ongoing enterprise risk management program."),
    ("Note", "Policy provisions governing communications and social media apply to content posted under pseudonyms or anonymous accounts to the extent that the author is identifiable as an employee or the content is reasonably attributable to the Company's employment relationship."),
    ("Reminder", "Legal advice about the interpretation of this policy or its application to a specific situation is available through the Office of General Counsel. Employees are not entitled to receive individual legal advice from the Company's attorneys, who represent the Company, not individual employees."),
    ("Exception", "This policy does not apply to activities conducted under a formal research protocol approved by an Institutional Review Board, to the extent that such protocol conflicts with specific provisions of this policy. Employees engaged in IRB-approved research should notify HR of any applicable conflicts."),
    ("Caution", "Employees who hold fiduciary roles — including plan trustees, investment committee members, and benefit plan administrators — are subject to heightened legal obligations under ERISA and other applicable law that are independent of and in addition to the standards established in this policy."),
    ("Important", "Employees who receive subpoenas, civil investigative demands, regulatory inquiries, or requests from law enforcement agencies that relate to Company business must immediately forward those documents to the Office of General Counsel without responding or producing documents independently."),
    ("Note", "The Company maintains a whistleblower protection log that records the outcome of all reports made through internal channels. This log is reviewed semi-annually by the Chief Compliance Officer and is available for inspection by the Company's auditors upon request."),
    ("Reminder", "Employees in multi-state roles or who work in more than one Company location during a calendar year may be subject to the most protective provisions of each applicable state's law. HR will provide state-specific supplemental guidance to employees in those situations upon request."),
    ("Exception", "Compensation provisions in this policy that reference market data or published benchmarks are updated at least every three years to reflect current compensation survey data. The specific survey sources used are identified in the Compensation Policy Appendix, available from Compensation on request."),
    ("Caution", "Documents, emails, and other records created in anticipation of litigation or a regulatory investigation may be subject to a legal hold that suspends normal document retention and destruction schedules. Employees who receive a legal hold notice must preserve all responsive documents and may not delete them under any circumstances until the hold is released."),
]

